Rule 9 (If out is provided, its memory layout should be compatible with the input's memory layout, which is hard to represent without more specific functions. This can be approximated by checking the number of elements)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= if v_2 ≠ "none" then (shape(v_1,0)*shape(v_1,1) = shape(v_2,0)*shape(v_2,1))
>>
Rule 10 (If out is provided, its requires_grad must be false)
{v_2 : tensor} |= if v_2 ≠ "none" then false
>>
Rule 11 (If out is provided, it must have the same device as input.)
{v_2 : tensor} |= if v_2 ≠ "none" then true
>>
Rule 12 (If out is provided, it must not be complex if input is float)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= if v_2 ≠ "none" then (if (dtype_(v_1) = 6 ∨ dtype_(v_1) = 7 ∨ dtype_(v_1) = 8) then (dtype_(v_2) ≠ 9 ∧ dtype_(v_2) ≠ 10))
>>
Rule 13 (If out is provided, its layout (represented simplistically by the sum of dimension sizes) must be at least as large as input, when input is floating point)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : tensor} |= if v_2 ≠ "none" then (if (dtype_(v_1) = 6 ∨ dtype_(v_1) = 7 ∨ dtype_(v_1) = 8) then (shape(v_1,0)+shape(v_1,1) ≤ shape(v_2,0)+shape(v_2,1)))
>>
Rule 14 (If out is provided, number of elements in out should be greater or equal than number of elements in input.)
